In plain English:
  At tau=1.0 the dreamed ball's mass starts at corr 0.70 with the
  truth, is 0.41 by step 24 and 0.13 by step 150. It falls below 0.5
  at step 8.
  At step 150 the dreamed ball moves at 9.32 x the speed the law gives it.
  It correlates with the speed its colour implies at r = 0.32 (probe floor
  0.82 where that is defined).
  The dream stays inside +-25% of the law for 2 steps on average (the
  measuring probe's own floor is 128).  80% of the dreamed frames still
  contain a ball of roughly the right size.
  At tau=0.0 (deterministic) the same numbers are log-mass corr 0.82, speed ratio 0.84, speed horizon 32.
  The gap between the two rows is the cost of sampling, which is the whole
  finding: the model KNOWS the quantity is constant, and loses it anyway
  as soon as it has to feed on its own draws.

